{
  "case_id": "2020-18-956",
  "document_hash": "0ff88de86221334e2bd06763dfe399841d3b9d1e943b330f2e969f33c28d1b36",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Google LLC",
      "case_id": "2020-18-956",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0ff88de86221334e2bd06763dfe399841d3b9d1e943b330f2e969f33c28d1b36",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0ff88de86221-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. GOLDSTEIN: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: The merger doctrine resolved the copyrightability question in this case. Oracle has a copyright to the computer code in Java SE but not a patent. That means that the public, not Oracle, has the right to Java SE's function, and Oracle cannot leverage its copyright to create patent-like rights. Specifically, under the merger doctrine, there is no copyright protection for computer code that is the only way to perform those functions."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Google LLC",
      "case_id": "2020-18-956",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0ff88de86221334e2bd06763dfe399841d3b9d1e943b330f2e969f33c28d1b36",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0ff88de86221-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "With respect to fair use, the long-settled practice of reusing software interfaces is critical to modern interoperable computer software. Here, reusing the minimally creative declarations allowed the developers to write millions of creative applications that are used by more than a billion people."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Oracle",
      "case_id": "2020-18-956",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0ff88de86221334e2bd06763dfe399841d3b9d1e943b330f2e969f33c28d1b36",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0ff88de86221-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Section\n102(b), what it tells you is that you can't get\na copyright in the functionality of a computer\ncode. And there are so many things listed in\nSection 102(b), like method of operation,\nbecause Congress wanted to be encompassing. You\nget to copyright none of the functionality."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2020-18-956",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0ff88de86221334e2bd06763dfe399841d3b9d1e943b330f2e969f33c28d1b36",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0ff88de86221-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: My problem with\nyour argument is, what's your definition of\n\"interoperability\"? It seems one-directional.\nYou seem to define it as the extent to which\nexisting third-party applications can run on\nyour platform but not whether apps developed on\nyour platform can run on systems that use Java\nSE. So it's one way."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Oracle",
      "case_id": "2020-18-956",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0ff88de86221334e2bd06763dfe399841d3b9d1e943b330f2e969f33c28d1b36",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0ff88de86221-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "You cannot get copyright protection\nwith respect to any method of operation. This\nis plainly the method of operating Java SE."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Oracle",
      "case_id": "2020-18-956",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0ff88de86221334e2bd06763dfe399841d3b9d1e943b330f2e969f33c28d1b36",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0ff88de86221-0001-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I want to address the argument that it\nis sufficient that Google could write new\ndeclarations that would require developers to\nlearn new instructions and that we're\neffectively just stealing this efficient way of\ndoing it."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Google",
      "case_id": "2020-18-956",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0ff88de86221334e2bd06763dfe399841d3b9d1e943b330f2e969f33c28d1b36",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0ff88de86221-0001-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Legal principle 1: Congress defined\nliterary work to include software and granted\ncopyright protection as long as the code is\noriginal. Google conceded Oracle's code is\noriginal. That's the end of the question.\nGoogle asks this Court to carve out\ndeclaring code, but Congress rejected the very\ncarveout in multiple ways, including in its\ndefinition of computer program and by not\nincluding Google's carveout among the\nlimitations in Section 117."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2020-18-956",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0ff88de86221334e2bd06763dfe399841d3b9d1e943b330f2e969f33c28d1b36",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0ff88de86221-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: Yes. Thank you,\nMr. Chief Justice.\nMr. Rosenkranz, in your brief, you\nseem to be arguing for more than the declaring\ncode. If I'm right there, do we need to decide\nmore than that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Google",
      "case_id": "2020-18-956",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0ff88de86221334e2bd06763dfe399841d3b9d1e943b330f2e969f33c28d1b36",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0ff88de86221-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The code was never incorporated into a competing product. Instead, it was used to study, to figure out how the machine worked, and that was a transformative use."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2020-18-956",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0ff88de86221334e2bd06763dfe399841d3b9d1e943b330f2e969f33c28d1b36",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0ff88de86221-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: All right. Please\nassume with me the following: Assume that the\n-- what you read, the computer -- computer\nprograms which do something, after all, are\ncopyrightable, but then it says methods of\noperation are not, whether they're computer\nprograms or not."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Google",
      "case_id": "2020-18-956",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0ff88de86221334e2bd06763dfe399841d3b9d1e943b330f2e969f33c28d1b36",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0ff88de86221-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 54,
        "start": 54
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Programming Interface, of which the declaring code is a part, is not copyrightable. Implementing codes are."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Google",
      "case_id": "2020-18-956",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0ff88de86221334e2bd06763dfe399841d3b9d1e943b330f2e969f33c28d1b36",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0ff88de86221-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Third Circuit, the First Circuit, the Ninth Circuit, the Tenth Circuit, they all agree with that. No one draw that -- drew that distinction between implementing code and declaring code."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2020-18-956",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0ff88de86221334e2bd06763dfe399841d3b9d1e943b330f2e969f33c28d1b36",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0ff88de86221-0003-0000",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 65,
        "start": 65
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In the mid 1970s, Congress established a national commission to study problems related to copyright law and computer code. And in 1978, the Commission issued its report which is known as the CONTU report. It recommended that computer code continue to be eligible for copyright protection."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2020-18-956",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0ff88de86221334e2bd06763dfe399841d3b9d1e943b330f2e969f33c28d1b36",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0ff88de86221-0003-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 66,
        "start": 66
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "potential authors of computer code knew that their works could be freely copied, there would be a pronounced disincentive to creation."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2020-18-956",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0ff88de86221334e2bd06763dfe399841d3b9d1e943b330f2e969f33c28d1b36",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0ff88de86221-0003-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 68,
        "start": 68
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: Thank you, Chief Justice. Mr. Stewart, a couple of quick questions. One, do you think the Federal Circuit applied the proper review standard?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2020-18-956",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0ff88de86221334e2bd06763dfe399841d3b9d1e943b330f2e969f33c28d1b36",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0ff88de86221-0003-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 74,
        "start": 74
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Counsel, could you tell me why you think that Google's work was not transformative? It moved Java's platform from a PC, essentially, to mobile phones."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Goldstein",
      "case_id": "2020-18-956",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "0ff88de86221334e2bd06763dfe399841d3b9d1e943b330f2e969f33c28d1b36",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0ff88de86221-0004-0000",
      "kind": "allegation",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 86,
        "start": 86
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We are not -- Oracle does not get to claim as -- the exclusive right to a highly functional computer program without a patent. It gets to claim the words on the page. And if those are the only words on the page that will produce this result in the computer, they don't get that exclusive copyright."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2020-18-956",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "0ff88de86221334e2bd06763dfe399841d3b9d1e943b330f2e969f33c28d1b36",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0ff88de86221-0004-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 87,
        "start": 87
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I -- I've heard from the other side that, yes, that may be true, but this result is simply calling up a set of programs that were written by Java. And maybe at the beginning you could have done this in different ways with different divisions of tasks in a world with different call-up numbers. And there weren't people trained at that time. And copyright, you just heard quoted, runs from the beginning. What do you do about that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Goldstein",
      "case_id": "2020-18-956",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "0ff88de86221334e2bd06763dfe399841d3b9d1e943b330f2e969f33c28d1b36",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0ff88de86221-0004-0002",
      "kind": "allegation",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 92,
        "start": 92
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Yes, this is the exact argument that was made and rejected in the Court's Hana Financial decision, and that is, sure, some issues are decided very frequently on summary judgment, but that doesn't deem that there aren't other incredibly highly contested facts -- cases that arise in new environments, as I believe you pointed out earlier."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2020-18-956",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "0ff88de86221334e2bd06763dfe399841d3b9d1e943b330f2e969f33c28d1b36",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0ff88de86221-0004-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 97,
        "start": 97
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted. (Whereupon, at 11:36 a.m., the case was submitted.)"
    },
    {
